COMMAND NAME: mpp_dumpall

Writes out all databases and the Bizgres MPP system catalog to 
SQL script files, which can then be used to restore a Bizgres 
MPP system configuration, databases, and user data.

This script is located in $MPPHOME/bin

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mpp_dumpall [<database_name>] [-h <hostname>] [-p <port>] 
[-U <username>] [-W] [-a] [-d] [-D] [-i] [-n <namespace>] 
[-o] [-O] [-s] [-x] [--mpp-d=<directoryname>] [--mpp-r=<filename>]

*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mpp_dumpall utility dumps the contents of a Bizgres MPP system into a 
series of SQL script files, which can then be used to restore a Bizgres MPP 
system configuration, databases, and user data. During a dump operation, 
users will still have read-only access to the databases. However, write 
operations will be blocked during the dump operation.

The functionality of mpp_dumpall is analogous to PostgreSQLs pg_dumpall 
utility, which writes out (or dumps) the content of all databases into a 
script file. The script file contains SQL commands that can be used to 
restore the databases, data, and global objects such as users, groups, 
and access permissions.

The functionality of mpp_dumpall is modified to accommodate the distributed 
nature of a Bizgres MPP database. Keep in mind that a database in Bizgres MPP 
is actually comprised of several PostgreSQL instances (the master and all 
segments), each of which must be dumped individually. The mpp_dumpall utility 
takes care of dumping all of the individual instances across the system.

The mpp_dumpall utility performs the following actions and produces the 
following dump files:

ON THE MASTER HOST

* Dumps the Bizgres MPP schema and system catalog information into a SQL 
  file in the master data directory. The default naming convention of this 
  file is mpp_catalog_<master_id>_<timestamp>. This file can be used by the 
  mpprebuildcluster.sh script to recover a Bizgres MPP system in various 
  situations.

* Dumps CREATE DATABASE SQL statements into a file in the master data 
  directory. The default naming convention of this file is 
  mpp_cdatabase_<master_id>_<timestamp>. This statement can be run on 
  the master instance to recreate the user database(s).

* Dumps the user database schema(s) into a SQL file in the master data 
  directory. The default naming convention of this file is 
  mpp_dump_<master_id>_<timestamp>. This file is used by mpp_restore 
  to recreate the database schema(s).

* Creates a log file in the master data directory named 
  mpp_dump_status_<master_id>_<timestamp>.

* mpp_dumpall launches a mpp_dumpall_agent for each segment instance to 
  be backed up. mpp_dumpall_agent processes run on the segment hosts 
  and report status back to the mpp_dumpall process running on the master host.
 
ON THE SEGMENT HOSTS

* Dumps the user data for each segment instance into a SQL file in 
  the segment instances data directory. All segment instances on a 
  host (primary and mirror instances) are backed up. The default naming 
  convention of this file is mpp_dump_<seg_id>_<timestamp>. This file 
  is used by mpp_restore to recreate that particular segment of user data.

* Creates a log file in each segment instances data directory named 
  mpp_dump_status_<seg_id>_<timestamp>.

Note that the 14 digit timestamp is the number that uniquely identifies the 
backup job, and is part of the filename for each dump file created by a 
mpp_dumpall operation. This timestamp must be passed to the mpp_restore 
utility when restoring a Bizgres MPP database.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

<database_name>

If your Bizgres MPP system contains multiple databases, use this option 
to backup the named database only. The default is to backup all databases 
in the system. Note: This option does not currently work.

-h <hostname>

The host name of the MPP master host. If not provided, the value of $PGHOST 
or the local host is used. This option can also be specified as 
--host=hostname.

-p <port>
The MPP master port. If not provided, the value of $PGPORT or the port 
number provided at compile time is used. This option can also be specified 
as --port=port.

-U <username>
The database super user account name, for example bgadmin. If not provided, 
the value of $PGUSER or the current OS user name is used. This option can 
also be specified as --username.

-W

Forces a password prompt. This will happen automatically if the server 
requires password authentication.

-a

Dump only the user data, not the schema (data definitions). This option 
creates the user data dump files for the segment instances only. This option 
can also be specified as --data-only.

-d

Dumps the data as INSERT commands (rather than COPY). This will make 
restoration very slow, but is more portable to other database management 
systems. This option can also be specified as --inserts.

-D

Dump data as INSERT commands with explicit column names 
(INSERT INTO table (column, ...) VALUES ...). This will make restoration 
very slow, but is necessary if you want to rearrange the column ordering. 
This option can also be specified as --column-inserts or --attribute-inserts.

-i

Ignores a version mismatch between mpp_dumpall and the database server. 
This option can also be specified as --ignore-version.

-n <namespace>
Dumps the contents of the specified schema only. If this option is not 
specified, all non-system schemas in the target database will be dumped. 
This option can also be specified as --schema=schema. Caution: In this 
mode, mpp_dumpall makes no attempt to dump any other database objects 
that objects in the selected schema may depend upon. Therefore, there 
is no guarantee that the results of a single-schema dump can be 
successfully restored by themselves into a clean database.

-o

Dumps object identifiers (OIDs) for every table. Use of OIDs is not 
recommended in Bizgres MPP, so this option should not be used if 
restoring data to another Bizgres MPP installation. This option can 
also be specified as --oids.

-O

Do not output commands to set ownership of objects to match the 
original database. By default, mpp_dumpall issues SET SESSION AUTHORIZATION 
statements to set ownership of created schema elements. 
This option can also be specified as --no-owner.

-s

Dumps only the schema (data definitions) on the MPP master only, 
no user data is backed up on the segments. This option can also 
be specified as --schema-only.

-x

Prevents the dumping of access privileges (GRANT/REVOKE commands). 
This option can also be specified as --no-privileges or no-acl.

--mpp-d=directoryname

Specifies the relative or absolute path where the backup files will be 
placed on each host. If this is a relative path, it is considered to be 
relative to the data directory. If the path does not exist, it will be 
created, if possible. If not specified, defaults to the data directory 
of each instance to be backed up. Using this option may be desirable 
if each segment host has multiple segment instances  it will create 
the dump files in a centralized location.

--mpp-r=filename

Specifies the full path name where the backup job log files will be 
placed on each host. If not specified, defaults to the data directory 
of each instance to be backed up. Using this option may be desirable 
if each segment host has multiple segment instances  it will create 
the log files in a centralized location.

*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Back up all databases in a Bizgres MPP system:

mpp_dumpall


Back up all databases in a Bizgres MPP system, and create dump files 
in a centralized location on all hosts:

mpp_dumpall --mpp-d=/home/bgadmin/backups


Back up the Bizgres MPP system catalog and database schema only:

mpp_dumpall -s


